 ### Scale Featurization
-A method to provide scale information to the MLP by averaging and concatenating a featurized version of the downweighting factors $\omega_{j,\ell}$, ensuring features have consistent magnitude regardless of training progression [S31]. It scales the result by the standard deviation of values in $V_\ell$, padded with $\initval$ to prevent zero values, and applies a stop-gradient operator to the mean of the squared grid values to prevent optimization from indirectly modifying the scale-feature via $V_\ell$ [S31]. The featurized scale input is calculated as: $(2 \cdot \operatorname{mean}_j(\downweight_{j, \ell}) - 1)\sqrt{\initval^2 + \stopgrad(\operatorname{mean}(\grid_\ell^2))}$, where $\downweight_{j, \ell}$ is the downweighting factor, $\initval$ is the initialization magnitude of $V_\ell$, and $\grid_\ell$ represents the values in $V_\ell$ [S31]. An approximation for $\operatorname{erf}(x)$ is used for speed: $\operatorname{erf}(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x) \sqrt{1 - \exp(-(\sfrac{4}{\pi})x^2)}$ [S31]. The erf approximation has no discernible impact on quality and only a marginal impact on performance [S31].
+A method to provide scale information to the MLP by averaging and concatenating a featurized version of the downweighting factors $\omega_{j,\ell}$, ensuring features have consistent magnitude regardless of training progression [S34]. It scales the result by the standard deviation of values in $V_\ell$, padded with $\initval$ to prevent zero values, and applies a stop-gradient operator to the mean of the squared grid values to prevent optimization from indirectly modifying the scale-feature via $V_\ell$ [S34]. The featurized scale input is calculated as: $(2 \cdot \operatorname{mean}_j(\downweight_{j, \ell}) - 1)\sqrt{\initval^2 + \stopgrad(\operatorname{mean}(\grid_\ell^2))}$, where $\downweight_{j, \ell}$ is the downweighting factor, $\initval$ is the initialization magnitude of $V_\ell$, and $\grid_\ell$ represents the values in $V_\ell$ [S34]. An approximation for $\operatorname{erf}(x)$ is used for speed: $\operatorname{erf}(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x) \sqrt{1 - \exp(-(\sfrac{4}{\pi})x^2)}$ [S34]. The erf approximation has no discernible impact on quality and only a marginal impact on performance [S34].
 
